  "strategy": "1. **Approach and Rationale**  \n   The goal is to minimize the maximum overlap integral \\( C_5 = \\max_k \\int h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx \\) for a step function \\( h: [0,2] \\rightarrow [0,1] \\) with \\( \\int_0^2 h(x)dx = 1 \\). The previous approach combined evolutionary optimization with local refining, but we address the following bottlenecks:  \n   - **Dimensionality Growth**: Directly optimizing large \\( n \\) points (\\( \\sim 1000 \\)) is computationally expensive.  \n   - **Constraint Enforcement**: Handling the integral constraint and bounds \\( [0,1] \\) inefficiently in prior methods.  \n   - **Local Optima**: Evolutionary methods can get trapped in regions with suboptimal solutions.  \n   Our solution uses **dual-resolution optimization**: (1) A global search at medium resolution \\( n = 500 \\) to escape local optima and (2) Local polishing at the highest feasible resolution (\\( n=1000 \\)). We ensure feasibility through adaptive projection and balance exploration vs. exploitation using a probabilistic coordinate-descent method.  \n\n2. **Mathematical Specification**  \n   - **Variables**: \\( h \\in [0,1]^{n} \\) with \\( \\sum h_i \\cdot \\frac{2}{n} = 1 \\).  \n   - **Objective**:  \n     \\[\n     C_5 = \\frac{2}{n} \\cdot \\max_k \\sum_{i} h_i (1 - h_{i+k})\n     \\]  \n   - **Constraints**:\n     - Integral balance: \\( \\sum h_i = n/2 \\)  \n     - Bounds: \\( 0 \\leq h_i \\leq 1 \\)  \n   - **Smooth Approximation**:  \n     \\[\n     f_\\alpha(h) = \\min_{c} \\max_{k} \\left\\{ \\alpha (c - \\text{actual}\\_\\text{shift}\\_score) + f(c) \\right\\}\n     \\]  \n     for \\( \\alpha \\)-smoothed max operation. This penalizes large shifts while reducing non-convexity.\n\n3. **Implementation Procedure**  \n   **Phase 1: Global Binary Encoding**  \n   - Sample \\( h \\in \\{0,1\\}^{500} \\) with \\( \\sum h_i = 250 \\).  \n   - Objective: \\( f_\\alpha \\) evaluated using FFT-based autocorrelation.  \n\n   **Phase 2: Adaptive Projection & Local Refinement**  \n   - **Feasibility Projection**: Project \\( h \\) to \\( [0,1] \\) with sum \\( n/2 \\) using weighted averaging:  \n     \\[\n     h_i \\leftarrow \\frac{1}{T} \\sum_{t=1}^T h_i^\\tau\n     \\]  \n     for temperature schedule \\( T \\).  \n   - **Local Polishing**: Use coordinate descent with probabilistic updates:  \n     \\[\n     h_i \\rightarrow h_i + \\epsilon \\cdot \\text{clip}(-\\text{smooth\\_gradient}(h), 0, \\text{target}- \\text{current})\n     \\]  \n\n   **Phase 3: Multi-Resolution Transition**  \n   - Interpolate binary solution at \\( n=500 \\) to \\( n'=1000 \\) via piecewise linear interpolation.  \n   - Repair feasibility using self-normalizing projection:  \n     \\[\n     h_{i'} = h_i + \\epsilon \\cdot \\text{clip}(\\delta)\n     \\]  \n     with adaptive \\( \\epsilon \\).  \n\n4. **Search Choices & Variations**  \n   - **Binary vs. Continuous**: Use binary initial encoding for 500-point resolution; transition to continuous for 1000-point optimization.  \n   - **Variation 1**: Progressive binarization: Convert interpolated solutions to binary via thresholding.  \n   - **Variation 2**: Meta-Lamarckian learning: Learn adaptive parameters (\\( \\epsilon, \\alpha \\)) from past optimizations.  \n\n5. **Compute Budget & Robustness**  \n   - **Estimated Timings**:  \n     - \\( n=500 \\): 8 seconds/evaluation  \n     - \\( n=1000 \\): 25 seconds/evaluation  \n   - Schedule:  \n     - Initial global phase: 400 evaluations at \\( n=500 \\)  \n     - Local polish: 100 evaluations at \\( n=1000 \\)  \n   - Early Termination: If \\( C_5 \\) below 0.3805 detected, terminate and return.  \n   - **Fallback**: Use binary closest vector projection if interpolation fails.  \n\n6. **Validation & Return Contract**  \n   - **Feasibility Check**: \\( \\sum h_i \\cdot \\frac{2}{n} = 1 \\) and \\( 0 \\leq h_i \\leq 1 \\).  \n   - **Objective Score**: Compute \\( C_5 \\) using the provided evaluator.  \n   - **Fallback**: If optimization fails, return the binary projection of the interpolated parent from \\( n=500 \\to n=600 \\).  \n\n   **Output**: \\( (h, C_5, n) \\)",
